1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,620
It was the dot-com decade when Big and Clunky paved the way for small and smart.

2
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,580
Virtual pets sought electric sustenance.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:14,240
People didn't really know how much power was inside this thing.

4
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,080
A military mover invaded suburbia.

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:21,160
George Foreman plugged a little item to knock out the fat.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:23,080
I was a happy man.

7
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:27,520
And a guy named Tim wove a web of information that changed the world.

8
00:00:27,521 --> 00:00:31,260
Now, 90s Tech on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:50,700
The founder and CEO of the company that owns this warehouse, and another 26 like it, was working out of his garage in 1995.

10
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:55,920
Today, he's one of the richest men in the world.

11
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:04,820
The wake-up call was finding out that worldwide web usage was growing 2,300% a year.

12
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:06,040
That was in 1994.

13
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:12,280
And that really started me thinking, what kind of business could you build on the web?

14
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,560
Realizing cyberspace would be a great platform for commerce,

15
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:22,500
Jeff Bezos researched top-selling mail-order products and decided to create an online bookstore.

16
00:01:22,501 --> 00:01:31,560
Amazon.com opened its site to the world with this web page on July 16, 1995.

17
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:40,600
Offering more than one million titles, the virtual shop sold books in all 50 states and 45 other countries within the first month.

18
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:47,120
We had programmed a bell to ring every time there was an order.

19
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:51,120
And it only took a few days before the bell became annoying.

20
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,020
So it did grow fairly quickly.

21
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:57,640
First year's sales surpassed $15 million.

22
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:02,980
By the end of the 90s, Time Magazine named Bezos Person of the Year.

23
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:11,600
In early 1999, Jeff Bezos set a mission to grow his company from a books company to an internet retailer that could sell anything in the world.

24
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:19,840
I'm standing here in Fernley, Nevada, an 800,000 square foot facility that ships hundreds of thousands of units a day that was part of that vision.

25
00:02:19,841 --> 00:02:24,840
The facility was completed in January 1999.

26
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:30,840
With a few exceptions, the system put in place then is the system Amazon uses today.

27
00:02:30,841 --> 00:02:37,840
Thousands of workers and more than six miles of conveyor belts keep the cyber goods rolling off the shelves.

28
00:02:37,841 --> 00:02:42,840
In the beginning, pickers walked around with a little list of paper with the items that they needed for each of the customers.

29
00:02:42,841 --> 00:02:45,840
Today we use wireless RF scanner guns.

30
00:02:45,841 --> 00:02:48,720
While we do use library shelving, it's not Dewey Decimal System.

31
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,500
Basically, a person stowing the books can stow that book anywhere they like.

32
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:54,720
The picker is simply directed to that location.

33
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:58,340
Once picked, items head to sorting and packaging.

34
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:02,920
This part of our facility is where all the orders come together.

35
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:05,040
Each one of these chutes is a customer order.

36
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:10,140
So all the items are coming from all over this facility, sorted into a particular chute.

37
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,040
The key to this automated system is the tilt trays.

38
00:03:14,041 --> 00:03:19,040
Each item slips onto a tilt tray that carries it to the order's assigned chute.

39
00:03:19,041 --> 00:03:26,040
Since the tilt trays move at a uniform speed, the system calculates the exact travel time to the designated chute.

40
00:03:26,041 --> 00:03:31,040
At just the right moment, the tray releases the product.

41
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:36,040
When an order is complete, a light alerts the workers to box it up.

42
00:03:36,041 --> 00:03:43,040
I have to admit, when we opened this building in 1999, we didn't exactly know how to run all this equipment very effectively.

43
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:48,718
effectively. But today, Amazon has the process down to a precise science.

44
00:03:48,719 --> 00:03:50,040
And shipping from

45
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:56,537
27 individual warehouses, it's come a long way from its mid-90s garage roots.

46
00:03:56,538 --> 00:03:57,238
Raising

47
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:01,321
the initial startup money for Amazon.com was very difficult.

48
00:04:01,322 --> 00:04:02,960
The first question I had to

49
00:04:02,961 --> 00:04:10,500
answer for almost all of these investors, maybe all of them, is what's the Internet?

50
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:12,000
which was very normal at that time.

51
00:04:12,001 --> 00:04:14,500
Most people didn't really know what was going on with the Internet

52
00:04:14,501 --> 00:04:16,500
or what the World Wide Web was.

53
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,500
While many people use the terms Internet and World Wide Web interchangeably,

54
00:04:24,501 --> 00:04:27,500
the two are not synonymous.

55
00:04:27,501 --> 00:04:31,500
The Internet connects small computer networks to larger networks,

56
00:04:31,501 --> 00:04:35,500
and finally to a global system of computer networks around the world.

57
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:38,500
The World Wide Web, better known as the Web,

58
00:04:38,501 --> 00:04:43,500
Web is a system of interlinked computer documents that are accessed via the Internet.

59
00:04:43,501 --> 00:04:50,500
While the Web is a large portion of the Internet, the Internet is also used for e-mail, instant

60
00:04:50,501 --> 00:04:53,045
messaging and file transfer.

61
00:04:53,046 --> 00:04:58,500
The development of the Internet began in 1969, when the U.S.

62
00:04:58,501 --> 00:05:02,500
government's Advanced Research Projects Agency connected four of its research computers

63
00:05:02,501 --> 00:05:05,500
computers together through a 56K line.

64
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:10,500
Twenty-one years later, in the fall of 1990,

65
00:05:10,501 --> 00:05:14,500
there were 313,000 computers connected on the Internet.

66
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,500
But the U.S. government restricted the use of this primitive Internet

67
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:20,500
to research purposes only.

68
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,500
And unlike the graphics-laden environment we take for granted today,

69
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:28,500
the screen interface was a blank slate

70
00:05:28,501 --> 00:05:31,500
that required expert knowledge of text command.

71
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:35,740
the World Wide Web changed all that.

72
00:05:35,741 --> 00:05:40,780
While working at the European Organization for Nuclear Research, or CERN,

73
00:05:40,781 --> 00:05:43,180
British scientist Tim Berners-Lee

74
00:05:43,181 --> 00:05:48,620
saw the need for a user-friendly system for sharing information across the Internet.

75
00:05:48,621 --> 00:05:53,480
By combining hypertext, computer documents that link together,

76
00:05:53,481 --> 00:05:58,060
with the Internet, Berners-Lee created the Hypertext Transfer Protocol,

77
00:05:58,061 --> 00:06:00,060
or HTTP,

78
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:04,260
and coined the term World Wide Web.

79
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:10,020
And in the winter of 1990, he built the three tools necessary for a working web.

80
00:06:10,021 --> 00:06:15,120
The first web server or internet computer, a web browser or software application used

81
00:06:15,121 --> 00:06:21,220
to surf the web, and web pages, which describe the project.

82
00:06:21,221 --> 00:06:27,460
On August 6, 1991, Berners-Lee decided to give the World Wide Web free to the public

83
00:06:27,461 --> 00:06:29,860
so that it would continue to grow.

84
00:06:29,861 --> 00:06:32,860
revolutionizing the way we use computers and communicate.

85
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:39,720
So how is it that you can access billions of web pages online

86
00:06:39,721 --> 00:06:42,820
with just the click of a mouse?

87
00:06:42,821 --> 00:06:46,300
When you enter a URL or address on your web browser,

88
00:06:46,301 --> 00:06:50,460
it points to a specific web page on a web server.

89
00:06:50,461 --> 00:06:53,920
Routers direct the request from your computer across the network

90
00:06:53,921 --> 00:06:57,920
to the web server and then send the information back to display the web page.

91
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:04,800
And some advanced routers today can run at a capacity of up to 92 terabits per second.

92
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:11,420
That's two billion times faster than the original 56K line of 1969.

93
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,880
Although seven out of every ten Americans use the Internet,

94
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:21,020
North America ranks third behind Asia and Europe in web traffic.

95
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:29,500
By June 2007, more than one billion people, or about 17% of the world population,

96
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:33,560
had used the Internet.

97
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:39,920
Imagine when that number grows to 50% or 80% or 90% of the global population sharing their

98
00:07:39,921 --> 00:07:42,920
lives, sharing experience, sharing knowledge, sharing information.

99
00:07:42,921 --> 00:07:47,800
It fundamentally changes how we are as a society.

100
00:07:47,801 --> 00:07:52,260
While the web browser was the gateway to a world of information, you still needed to

101
00:07:52,261 --> 00:07:55,140
know the web address to access it.

102
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:58,360
So what if you didn't know the address?

103
00:07:58,361 --> 00:08:03,360
Google is a so-called crawler-based search engine

104
00:08:03,361 --> 00:08:05,360
that can direct you to pertinent websites

105
00:08:05,361 --> 00:08:07,360
by crawling the web and sorting through

106
00:08:07,361 --> 00:08:11,360
its increasingly massive amounts of information.

107
00:08:11,361 --> 00:08:13,360
Information on the Internet is growing at such an amazing rate

108
00:08:13,361 --> 00:08:15,360
that in about three years it's anticipated

109
00:08:15,361 --> 00:08:18,360
that it will double every 11 hours.

110
00:08:18,361 --> 00:08:20,360
So what happens when you use Google

111
00:08:20,361 --> 00:08:24,360
to search 90s pop star Britney Spears?

112
00:08:24,361 --> 00:08:26,360
The Google search engine uses spiders

113
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:32,360
and uses spiders that crawl the web, scanning each web page on a regular basis.

114
00:08:32,361 --> 00:08:37,360
This data is entered into an index, much like the index of a book.

115
00:08:37,361 --> 00:08:43,360
When you enter a search query, Google's software sips through the index and ranks the matches,

116
00:08:43,361 --> 00:08:47,360
returning results in order of relevance.

117
00:08:47,361 --> 00:08:54,360
And in this case, you get more than 49 million results in just .04 seconds.

118
00:08:54,361 --> 00:08:58,060
That's because Google has countless computers clustered around the world,

119
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,500
working in tandem to scour their index.

120
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:05,400
But this popular site wasn't always so expansive.

121
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:13,220
As you can see, it says the index contains about 25 million pages,

122
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,540
and that was big for that time.

123
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:18,140
And now we have billions and billions of pages.

124
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:21,420
Founded by two Stanford University students,

125
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:23,660
Larry Page and Sergey Brin,

126
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,100
Google launched in September 1998.

127
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:33,160
Google, G-O-O-G-O-L, is a very large number.

128
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:36,300
It is the digit one followed by 100 zeros.

129
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:44,500
And Google is a play on the word Google to depict our mission of capturing and organizing

130
00:09:44,501 --> 00:09:46,860
large and vast amounts of information.

131
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:50,660
Though Google won't release the amount of servers it has today,

132
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:56,080
Some industry experts estimate there may be as many as 450,000.

133
00:09:56,980 --> 00:10:01,980
But back in 1998, Google had just 30 server racks like this one.

134
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:06,780
Each rack held 80 computers running on Intel Pentium II chips.

135
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:14,620
Since their data center charged by the square foot, they crammed the computers in four to a shelf.

136
00:10:14,621 --> 00:10:21,620
By the end of 1998, Google was answering 10,000 search queries each day.

137
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:26,620
Just two years later, that number had grown to 100 million.

138
00:10:26,621 --> 00:10:31,620
But the 90s brought many more Internet innovations.

139
00:10:31,621 --> 00:10:41,620
In 1996, e-mail became the main source of communication in the United States, eclipsing both the telephone and standard mail.

140
00:10:41,621 --> 00:10:47,960
The 90s also ushered in AOL, instant messenger, chat rooms.

141
00:10:47,961 --> 00:10:50,300
The options were endless.

142
00:10:50,301 --> 00:10:54,500
And the notion of web surfing literally came up where people would surf for hours from

143
00:10:54,501 --> 00:10:59,920
site to site to site.

144
00:10:59,921 --> 00:11:04,440
And the portable computers of the 90s let us surf the web from anywhere.

145
00:11:04,441 --> 00:11:08,060
But this innovation traces its roots to the 80s.

146
00:11:08,061 --> 00:11:13,520
As preposterous as this looks to us today, this is the beginning of the portable computer

147
00:11:13,521 --> 00:11:15,940
and this is the Osborne 1.

148
00:11:15,941 --> 00:11:24,740
This is a CRT screen, it's five inches diagonal, it can display about 50 by 24 characters,

149
00:11:24,741 --> 00:11:26,860
no graphics whatsoever.

150
00:11:26,861 --> 00:11:37,820
You would fold the keyboard up into the case like this, snap it down, and it became 25

151
00:11:37,821 --> 00:11:42,140
pound boat anchor kind of thing, but you could take your computer with you for the first time.

152
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:46,140
But by the beginning of the 90s...

153
00:11:46,141 --> 00:11:53,140
This is 1991 technology, the PowerBook 170, which had everything you'd want in a modern

154
00:11:53,141 --> 00:11:54,308
notebook computer.

155
00:11:54,309 --> 00:11:58,720
It has a nice screen, has the trackball, and notice how the keyboard

156
00:11:58,721 --> 00:12:04,160
has actually moved back to give you these palm rests here. It's really the defining

157
00:12:04,161 --> 00:12:09,800
embodiment of everything notebook that follows it in the 1990s.

158
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:13,380
The 90s even miniaturized the cell phone.

159
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:20,200
We're shrinking the processor, the memory, the display, the battery especially,

160
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:26,340
adding new features like this flip phone feature to channel the Star Trek communicator.

161
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:32,340
Finally, by the end of the 1990s, you have phones that are so small and so inexpensive

162
00:12:32,341 --> 00:12:35,340
that they're everywhere, just about everybody owns one.

163
00:12:35,341 --> 00:12:38,340
The 90s also saw the marriage of the cell phone

164
00:12:38,341 --> 00:12:42,340
with the laptop computer, creating the smartphone.

165
00:12:42,341 --> 00:12:46,340
This is the Simon from IBM in Bell South.

166
00:12:46,341 --> 00:12:49,340
It is the world's first smartphone.

167
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:52,340
It's really the precursor to what we think of today as the Blackberry.

168
00:12:52,341 --> 00:12:54,340
It had a calendar and all the other little things

169
00:12:54,341 --> 00:12:57,340
that we think of as standard features.

170
00:12:57,341 --> 00:13:01,340
But at the time, the idea of carrying this much power

171
00:13:01,341 --> 00:13:03,840
power in your hand really was revolutionary.

172
00:13:03,841 --> 00:13:09,140
While the creation of the World Wide Web transformed the computer into a new entertainment

173
00:13:09,141 --> 00:13:14,740
and information medium in the 90s, the use of computers in virtual pet toys created a

174
00:13:14,741 --> 00:13:21,360
national security threat.

175
00:13:21,361 --> 00:13:27,400
In 2007, over 170 billion email messages were sent per day.

176
00:13:27,401 --> 00:13:30,440
That's almost 2 million emails every second.

177
00:13:30,441 --> 00:13:34,220
70% of them were spam and viruses.

178
00:13:34,221 --> 00:13:36,760
90s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

179
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:50,000
RoboCup 2007.

180
00:13:50,001 --> 00:13:52,780
With no remote controls or human intervention,

181
00:13:52,781 --> 00:13:56,400
eight AIBO robotic dogs compete in four-on-four soccer

182
00:13:56,401 --> 00:13:58,900
competition.

183
00:13:58,901 --> 00:14:00,860
thanks to the technology of the 90s.

184
00:14:03,460 --> 00:14:05,900
So this is the first AIBO introduced in 1999.

185
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:08,640
This is the 110 and 111 series.

186
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:12,060
AIBO is actually an acronym for artificial intelligence bot,

187
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:14,640
but AIBO in Japanese also means PAL.

188
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:17,020
In 1999, when they came out, they were $2,500.

189
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:23,800
In June 1999, the first batch of 3,000 AIBOs sold out in just 20 minutes.

190
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:25,700
And by the end of the year,

191
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:28,820
Sony had produced and sold an additional 60,000.

192
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:37,040
IBOs are called autonomous robots because they're able to learn and mature based on external stimuli from their owner and surroundings.

193
00:14:38,480 --> 00:14:46,320
So if you wanted it to play with the pink ball a lot and you kept telling it was a good dog when it played with the pink ball, it would learn that that's a good thing.

194
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:51,620
As IBOs mature, they save all of their new memories, just like a computer.

195
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:55,080
And all that junk is stored in his trunk.

196
00:14:55,940 --> 00:14:59,700
All of the brains of the AIBOs look essentially the same.

197
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:02,680
They're just memory sticks, just like you have in a modern digital camera.

198
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:06,560
And actually, the original AIBOs only had up to an 8 megabyte of storage.

199
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:11,260
The RoboDog's joints allowed for 20 different movements.

200
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,400
Housed in his head was a sensor to react to touch,

201
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:16,000
a microphone for hearing,

202
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:18,140
a video camera for vision,

203
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:20,100
and a distance detector.

204
00:15:20,101 --> 00:15:26,140
The Aibo was the culmination of the virtual pet boom of the 90s.

205
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:30,180
What got it started was the Tamagotchi.

206
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:36,520
A Tamagotchi is a virtual pet housed in an egg-shaped casing.

207
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:41,800
It means egg, and in Japanese, it's a cute, lovable egg.

208
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:47,660
Tamagotchi exploded into popularity in the United States in 1997.

209
00:15:47,661 --> 00:15:53,260
It was just amazing. People were lined up in the streets to see this.

210
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:57,340
In its peak year, Tamagotchi sold over 40 million units worldwide.

211
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:04,040
And I think what's so amazing is that if you translate that, that's 15 units every minute.

212
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:09,220
Three buttons allowed the owner of the pet to feed it, play games with it,

213
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:11,660
check its hunger and happiness levels,

214
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:16,540
and even perform a pet owner's least favorite task, cleaning up after it.

215
00:16:17,660 --> 00:16:24,660
And if not taken care of properly, your Tamagotchi would actually kick the bucket, just like a real pet.

216
00:16:24,661 --> 00:16:33,660
Two button-sized 1.5-volt batteries kept the virtual creature running, and all the commands ran through a computer chip.

217
00:16:33,661 --> 00:16:40,660
And it had nearly double the power of the Osborne 1 portable computer of 1981.

218
00:16:40,661 --> 00:16:45,460
We created the virtual pet.

219
00:16:45,461 --> 00:16:49,300
It was something that was never done before.

220
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:52,080
But just one year later,

221
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:55,700
the little digital critters had a tangible furry competitor.

222
00:16:57,020 --> 00:17:00,300
No discussion of the 1990s would be complete

223
00:17:00,301 --> 00:17:02,180
without talking about Furby.

224
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:04,440
Introduced in 1998,

225
00:17:04,860 --> 00:17:08,960
it was the world's first autonomous robotic pet.

226
00:17:08,961 --> 00:17:15,440
So as a robot, it's able to move things like its ears and its mouth and its eyes.

227
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:19,200
It also speaks, and it speaks a language called furbish.

228
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:28,500
The more you played with your furry, furby friend, it would speak less furbish and seem to learn English.

229
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:37,920
What it really did was gradually reveal up to 100 pre-programmed English words.

230
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:45,440
Because of this, there was a common misconception that Furbies repeated the words said around them.

231
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:50,620
And some government officials thought the toy could threaten national security.

232
00:17:53,100 --> 00:18:01,940
In fact, the National Security Agency in Maryland banned its employees from bringing the little guy into its buildings on January 13, 1999.

233
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:06,140
People didn't really know what was going on here.

234
00:18:06,220 --> 00:18:08,520
They didn't know how much power was inside this thing.

235
00:18:08,521 --> 00:18:09,760
so it scared some people.

236
00:18:09,761 --> 00:18:11,220
Be scared.

237
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:14,900
Despite its treasonous reputation,

238
00:18:14,901 --> 00:18:18,360
Furby sold more than 15 million units by the end of the 90s.

239
00:18:21,560 --> 00:18:25,000
The Furby cost about $35 in 1998,

240
00:18:25,001 --> 00:18:27,880
which is pretty expensive for a toy.

241
00:18:27,881 --> 00:18:30,980
But when you take apart a Furby and look inside,

242
00:18:30,981 --> 00:18:32,740
you realize why it cost so much.

243
00:18:32,741 --> 00:18:36,180
It really was a miracle of modern technology.

244
00:18:36,181 --> 00:18:41,180
The Furby came fully equipped with touch sensors on the front and back,

245
00:18:41,181 --> 00:18:45,180
a microphone to pick up sound, a sensor to detect light,

246
00:18:45,181 --> 00:18:48,180
an infrared port to communicate with other Furbies,

247
00:18:48,181 --> 00:18:51,180
and even a sensor that could detect upside-down movement.

248
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:59,180
One of the things that let them keep the cost down was a design feature

249
00:18:59,181 --> 00:19:05,180
that used just one electric motor that drove a cam system.

250
00:19:05,181 --> 00:19:11,860
So this single motor with the cams can drive the ears, the eyelids, the mouth,

251
00:19:11,861 --> 00:19:16,560
and the ability of the Furby to tip up and kind of move forward.

252
00:19:16,561 --> 00:19:22,000
The microprocessor has about four times the power of the Apollo moon lander.

253
00:19:22,001 --> 00:19:28,240
It's an amazing amount of processing power to fit into such a small and inexpensive package.

254
00:19:28,241 --> 00:19:33,840
Furbies, Tamagotchis and Iboes may have had a leg up in the world of toys.

255
00:19:35,180 --> 00:19:41,860
But in the mid-90s, it was the George Foreman lean, mean, fat-reducing grilling machine that

256
00:19:41,861 --> 00:19:43,860
really delivered a knockout punch.

257
00:19:43,861 --> 00:19:47,640
I'd fallen in love originally with the grill because I could get up early in the morning

258
00:19:47,641 --> 00:19:51,060
and have actually salmon and bagels.

259
00:19:51,061 --> 00:19:55,080
And I could actually put the bagel on the grill, turn one this way and the other the

260
00:19:55,081 --> 00:19:56,080
opposite.

261
00:19:56,081 --> 00:19:59,740
I would grill on both sides and then have the salmon ready, put a little cream cheese.

262
00:19:59,741 --> 00:20:00,740
Whoooom!

263
00:20:00,741 --> 00:20:01,740
I was a happy man.

264
00:20:01,741 --> 00:20:04,780
While similar hamburger grills were available at the time,

265
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:08,820
the George Foreman grill introduced and patented a sloped design

266
00:20:08,821 --> 00:20:11,160
that allowed the fat to drip away from the meat.

267
00:20:12,180 --> 00:20:17,280
That was just what two-time world heavyweight boxing champion George Foreman was looking for.

268
00:20:18,080 --> 00:20:21,060
Everybody was calling me the man Mr. Cheeseburger,

269
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:24,580
and I'd made a joke because everybody laughed about my weight

270
00:20:24,581 --> 00:20:30,440
that I could go and eat a dozen cheeseburgers and then become heavyweight champ of the world.

271
00:20:30,441 --> 00:20:36,680
So now, how could I get high-protein food and with little fat the George Foreman grill?

272
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:41,580
In 1995, we introduced our GR-10, which was a two-hamburger size.

273
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:43,360
It retailed at $39.99.

274
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:48,420
By the end of the 90s, more than 14 million grills had flown off the shelves.

275
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:53,740
Though the size had grown to hold up to eight burgers, the technology remained the same.

276
00:20:55,040 --> 00:20:58,560
This is the original construction that was used for these grills.

277
00:20:58,561 --> 00:21:01,380
We have a heating element that's pressed into the die cast,

278
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:06,580
and the thermostat here maintains the temperature on the cooking surface

279
00:21:06,581 --> 00:21:08,980
at approximately 350 degrees Fahrenheit.

280
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:11,960
And that's a great temperature for cooking hamburgers.

281
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:16,260
A floating hinge ensures the meat, no matter how thick,

282
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:18,920
stays in close contact with both sides of the grill.

283
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:26,740
For Salton, this simple yet revolutionary grill was almost the one that got away.

284
00:21:26,741 --> 00:21:29,460
No one was interested in the grill.

285
00:21:29,800 --> 00:21:31,880
But a gentleman came to me and he said,

286
00:21:31,960 --> 00:21:33,380
I think I have a customer for it.

287
00:21:33,580 --> 00:21:35,420
And I said, you know, I can't sell it.

288
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:37,080
So if you want it, it's yours.

289
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,800
The potential buyer was none other than George Foreman.

290
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:45,020
I sat there and looked at it for days and months.

291
00:21:45,280 --> 00:21:47,460
So finally my wife ate some food already.

292
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:48,360
The grease went away.

293
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:50,480
I was afraid of that because too much,

294
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:51,600
you lose so many juices,

295
00:21:52,100 --> 00:21:53,640
it's not going to be juice anymore to me.

296
00:21:53,740 --> 00:21:55,220
But the company found out it was just great.

297
00:21:55,221 --> 00:21:56,980
Oh, that looks good.

298
00:21:57,140 --> 00:22:00,140
George didn't want to be marketing it himself.

299
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:06,300
And as it turned out, the advice came to him that Salton would be the best company to do it.

300
00:22:06,380 --> 00:22:08,220
So it came right back to me.

301
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:12,220
And after that, it was like a Japanese bullet train.

302
00:22:13,420 --> 00:22:17,760
Salton has sold more than 90 million George Foreman grills in just 12 years.

303
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:20,220
Over $5 billion worth.

304
00:22:21,560 --> 00:22:25,000
The George Foreman grill may have revolutionized the act of grilling.

305
00:22:25,220 --> 00:22:32,860
But some other sizzling technologies of the 90s forever changed the way we see and when we see TV.

306
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:38,240
Not all toys had to be robotic to be popular in the 90s.

307
00:22:38,780 --> 00:22:45,820
The low-tech Beanie Babies introduced in 1993 sold more than 100 million units in just three years.

308
00:22:47,220 --> 00:22:49,900
90s Tech will return on Modern Marvelous.

309
00:22:49,901 --> 00:22:59,880
Step into any electronic store and you'll be surrounded by indispensable digital gadgetry spawned in the 90s.

310
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:06,060
The digital revolution of the 1990s not only brought us information overload,

311
00:23:06,600 --> 00:23:10,360
but also gave us countless ways to extend our inner couch potato.

312
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:14,040
Case in point, the DVD.

313
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:17,380
Introduced in December 1995.

314
00:23:17,381 --> 00:23:22,380
At first glance, DVDs seem very similar to CDs.

315
00:23:22,381 --> 00:23:28,380
They're the same diameter and thickness, and their digital data is encoded in a long spiral

316
00:23:28,381 --> 00:23:31,380
of microscopic pits and bumps.

317
00:23:31,381 --> 00:23:38,380
But at 4.7 gigabytes of capacity, a DVD can hold almost seven times more data than a CD.

318
00:23:38,381 --> 00:23:43,380
That's because the pits and bumps are four times smaller on a DVD, making the spiral

319
00:23:43,381 --> 00:23:46,380
tighter and longer.

320
00:23:46,381 --> 00:23:50,220
And unlike a CD, a DVD can have up to four layers of data,

321
00:23:51,820 --> 00:23:56,760
increasing its capacity to almost 26 times more than a CD.

322
00:23:58,200 --> 00:24:03,160
And though DVDs are three times smaller than the analog laser discs pioneered in the 80s,

323
00:24:04,160 --> 00:24:06,400
they can hold more than double the data.

324
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:09,000
The payoff for consumers?

325
00:24:10,180 --> 00:24:15,580
For the first time, it was possible to fit an entire movie, and then some, onto a digital disc.

326
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:21,020
But that was only because of another 90s advancement, digital compression.

327
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:25,680
A movie would need about 40 times more space.

328
00:24:25,800 --> 00:24:30,780
The disc would be, you know, three feet in diameter or something if it held a movie in its native format.

329
00:24:30,920 --> 00:24:38,300
So what they do is compress the movie by a factor of about 40 to 1 using something called MPEG-2 compression.

330
00:24:38,301 --> 00:24:46,520
MPEG-2 compression reduces the size of a video file by reusing any identical video information.

331
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,620
Rather than storing all the data from each frame of video,

332
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:57,180
digital compression lets one frame of video borrow from the previous frame any information that didn't change.

333
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:04,940
That means that each new frame contains only the data that relates to how the picture is changed from the previous frame.

334
00:25:04,941 --> 00:25:11,940
By 1995, the MPEG-2 had become the standard format for video compression.

335
00:25:11,941 --> 00:25:17,940
And DVD players came fully equipped with an MPEG-2 decoder.

336
00:25:17,941 --> 00:25:22,940
Underneath the DVD, there is a laser.

337
00:25:22,941 --> 00:25:28,940
It's reading the data that's on the DVD, pulling it off to this board,

338
00:25:28,941 --> 00:25:36,180
which contains the computer technology that's necessary to decompress that MPEG-2 stream of data

339
00:25:36,181 --> 00:25:39,480
and make it possible for it to drive a television.

340
00:25:40,620 --> 00:25:44,480
DVDs ushered in an era of feature-packed special edition home videos.

341
00:25:44,860 --> 00:25:46,100
Could it possibly get any better?

342
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:52,600
How about the ability to pause and rewind live TV?

343
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:58,920
In March 1999, the TiVo Digital Video Recorder, or DVR, did just that.

344
00:25:58,940 --> 00:26:04,860
People in the late 90s couldn't imagine having the ability to pause, control live TV,

345
00:26:05,380 --> 00:26:08,840
and have all of their favorite shows waiting for them when they got home.

346
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:16,220
By the end of 2000, more than 150,000 TiVos had invaded households nationwide.

347
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:20,020
Today, that number has surpassed one million.

348
00:26:21,380 --> 00:26:24,840
So this is Modern Marvels, and it is recording right now.

349
00:26:24,841 --> 00:26:28,720
and I can watch it, I can fast forward it.

350
00:26:29,720 --> 00:26:33,620
The green bar shows me exactly how much of this show has been saved

351
00:26:33,621 --> 00:26:37,900
and I see a part that interests me, I can stop, I can watch that.

352
00:26:40,180 --> 00:26:44,820
At the time, one of the things that enabled TiVo to be the product that it was

353
00:26:44,821 --> 00:26:48,780
is that we were able to find a real-time MPEG encoder chip

354
00:26:48,781 --> 00:26:51,840
that could take regular analog television

355
00:26:51,841 --> 00:26:54,280
and turn it into digital television in real time.

356
00:26:54,281 --> 00:27:01,700
From there, the video is sent through this chip, which is a custom chip that we here at TiVo developed that we call the Media Switch.

357
00:27:02,420 --> 00:27:05,740
And the Media Switch writes the video to the disk drive.

358
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:13,640
With a 14-gigabyte capacity, the first TiVo can record one show at a time and store up to 14 hours of programming.

359
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:19,100
Today, TiVos can record two shows at once and store up to 300 hours.

360
00:27:20,640 --> 00:27:23,600
You can even transfer recorded programs to your computer.

361
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:28,340
listen to your music library, and create photo slideshows on the device.

362
00:27:30,420 --> 00:27:36,020
Of course, digital photo slideshows on TiVo wouldn't be possible without the digital camera.

363
00:27:38,540 --> 00:27:45,780
And the very first completely digital consumer camera can be traced back to the DICAM Model 1, made in 1990.

364
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:53,140
Now, this is pathetic by today's standards. That's really the only word possible.

365
00:27:53,141 --> 00:27:56,185
For one thing, it's black and white.

366
00:27:56,186 --> 00:28:03,120
For another thing, it only has 376 by 240 pixels or 0.09 megapixels of resolution.

367
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:10,320
And it's almost completely tethered to your computer because flash memory hasn't really become possible yet.

368
00:28:10,940 --> 00:28:11,980
Smile, Furby!

369
00:28:12,620 --> 00:28:16,160
The Dicam could store 32 black and white compressed images.

370
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:23,080
It wasn't until four years later that the Apple Quick Take 100 became the first affordable color camera.

371
00:28:23,140 --> 00:28:29,140
Now you can see that people hadn't yet standardized the shape yet, and this looks more like a

372
00:28:29,141 --> 00:28:32,900
set of binoculars than it does a digital camera.

373
00:28:32,901 --> 00:28:39,540
It's 640 by 480 or .3 megapixels, and it has flash memory, but it only has enough memory

374
00:28:39,541 --> 00:28:42,860
to store eight full resolution images.

375
00:28:42,861 --> 00:28:50,740
Then, just one year later, the Casio QV10 set the standard with its liquid crystal display.

376
00:28:50,741 --> 00:28:57,160
By 1995, the digital camera has evolved into the form factor that we're used to today.

377
00:28:57,380 --> 00:29:03,540
So it's got a lens, it's got the camera, and it's got an LCD on the back

378
00:29:03,541 --> 00:29:08,020
that lets us see the pictures as we're taking them and review the pictures that are in memory.

379
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:14,220
Modern 8 megapixel cameras are a far cry from the original digital cameras of the 90s.

380
00:29:14,221 --> 00:29:21,220
Just watch what happens when we compare today's image to the .09 megapixel limit of 1990.

381
00:29:34,220 --> 00:29:37,220
In 1994, satellite TV went digital.

382
00:29:37,221 --> 00:29:44,220
Direct TV was at the forefront of digital broadcast satellite technology, or DBS.

383
00:29:44,221 --> 00:29:49,220
We had a liftoff of the Direct TV satellite.

384
00:29:49,221 --> 00:29:54,220
Before DBS, consumer satellite dishes were as large as a hot tub,

385
00:29:54,221 --> 00:29:56,220
and no programming existed.

386
00:29:56,221 --> 00:30:00,220
Now TV lovers had dishes the size of a pizza

387
00:30:00,221 --> 00:30:02,220
and a set package of programming.

388
00:30:02,221 --> 00:30:05,220
There were a lot of people that didn't believe this was going to work.

389
00:30:05,221 --> 00:30:09,035
going to work. In fact, they thought DBS stood for don't be stupid.

390
00:30:09,036 --> 00:30:10,800
But the digital part is the key

391
00:30:10,801 --> 00:30:11,501
element.

392
00:30:11,502 --> 00:30:16,040
It allows us to do the compression, which allows us to have many more channels in a

393
00:30:16,041 --> 00:30:16,754
transponder.

394
00:30:16,755 --> 00:30:21,040
We launched with 70 channels and quickly grew to 200 within a few years.

395
00:30:21,760 --> 00:30:26,240
In addition to digital compression, the advent of higher-powered satellites in the early 90s

396
00:30:26,241 --> 00:30:28,700
made the smaller dishes possible.

397
00:30:28,701 --> 00:30:35,700
The DirecTV Los Angeles Broadcast Center monitors up to 1,600 channels.

398
00:30:35,701 --> 00:30:40,700
We're now in the DirecTV Operations Center at our Los Angeles Broadcast Facility.

399
00:30:40,701 --> 00:30:43,700
This is pretty much the way we built it in 1999.

400
00:30:43,701 --> 00:30:47,700
The signals that we receive from the outside are brought into this room

401
00:30:47,701 --> 00:30:50,700
and displayed on the monitors you see behind me.

402
00:30:50,701 --> 00:30:52,700
So this would look like a really cool job,

403
00:30:52,701 --> 00:30:55,700
but fundamentally the people aren't really watching television.

404
00:30:55,701 --> 00:31:00,700
They're watching for television to make sure that everything is going properly.

405
00:31:00,701 --> 00:31:03,700
Once the signals have been compressed and encrypted,

406
00:31:03,701 --> 00:31:10,700
the broadcast center beams them to DirecTV satellites, 22,300 miles above Earth.

407
00:31:10,701 --> 00:31:14,700
The satellites pick up the signal with an onboard dish,

408
00:31:14,701 --> 00:31:18,700
amplify it, and use another dish to beam it back to Earth,

409
00:31:18,701 --> 00:31:22,700
where the viewer satellite dish captures the signal.

410
00:31:22,701 --> 00:31:29,700
The Dish passes the signal along to the receiver that transmits it onto your TV.

411
00:31:29,701 --> 00:31:35,700
When DirecTV launched in 1994, some TV lovers couldn't wait.

412
00:31:35,701 --> 00:31:38,700
Within the first year we had a million subscribers.

413
00:31:38,701 --> 00:31:43,700
By the end of the 90s we had 8 million, and today we're at over 16 million subscribers.

414
00:31:43,701 --> 00:31:50,700
From the DVD to the DVR to the Dish, our digital devotion holds strong.

415
00:31:50,701 --> 00:31:54,720
But another gotta-have gizmo from the 90s really took us by storm.

416
00:31:55,800 --> 00:31:58,700
Today, we'd be lost without it. Literally.

417
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:04,500
Since TiVo is a subscription service,

418
00:32:05,340 --> 00:32:08,640
the company is able to anonymously gather its users' viewing habits.

419
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:11,760
The most rewound TiVo moment in history

420
00:32:11,761 --> 00:32:14,800
was Janet Jackson's wardrobe malfunction

421
00:32:14,801 --> 00:32:18,240
during the Super Bowl halftime show in 2004.

422
00:32:19,120 --> 00:32:21,920
90s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

423
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:27,540
January 17, 1991.

424
00:32:28,380 --> 00:32:29,780
Operation Desert Storm.

425
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:36,760
Some of our favorite 90s technologies got their start as national defense projects.

426
00:32:38,300 --> 00:32:40,349
And during the Gulf War crisis, U.S.

427
00:32:40,350 --> 00:32:43,080
troops were fully equipped with one defense tool

428
00:32:43,081 --> 00:32:48,280
that allowed them to navigate the vast, seemingly endless desert of Iraq with precision and ease.

429
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:53,240
The 90s technology that made it all possible

430
00:32:53,241 --> 00:32:58,940
was the United States government's GPS, or Global Positioning System.

431
00:32:59,620 --> 00:33:04,740
The Global Positioning System really proved its operational and military utility

432
00:33:04,741 --> 00:33:10,340
during the Desert Storm War and enabled our soldiers to navigate the desert for the first time.

433
00:33:10,341 --> 00:33:17,860
The United States Department of Defense began developing GPS in 1972 to track the location

434
00:33:17,861 --> 00:33:23,420
of its military units and to provide precision weapon delivery.

435
00:33:23,421 --> 00:33:27,820
And a boost in funding was granted to provide the addition of sensors on the satellites

436
00:33:27,821 --> 00:33:33,300
that could detect and locate nuclear detonations by foreign governments.

437
00:33:33,301 --> 00:33:43,260
On July 17, 1995, the $14 billion global positioning system reached full operational capability.

438
00:33:43,261 --> 00:33:47,420
It's the world's largest constellation of military satellites.

439
00:33:47,421 --> 00:33:54,240
At any given time, there are at least 24 operational satellites in semi-synchronized orbit, 12,600

440
00:33:54,241 --> 00:33:56,600
miles above the Earth.

441
00:33:56,601 --> 00:34:01,320
That means each satellite revolves around the Earth twice a day.

442
00:34:01,321 --> 00:34:03,680
They're distributed equally among six orbital planes,

443
00:34:03,940 --> 00:34:06,800
so that at least six satellites are within line of sight

444
00:34:06,801 --> 00:34:08,620
from almost anywhere on Earth's surface.

445
00:34:10,740 --> 00:34:12,060
What can it do for you?

446
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:14,280
This is from the mid-'90s.

447
00:34:14,380 --> 00:34:18,240
It was 96-ish when this receiver came out,

448
00:34:18,320 --> 00:34:21,780
so it knows that we are at a certain location,

449
00:34:21,781 --> 00:34:23,900
and it'll actually map it as we walk around.

450
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:27,340
Now I'm standing in a completely new spot.

451
00:34:28,120 --> 00:34:30,680
So at this point, I've moved several hundred feet

452
00:34:30,681 --> 00:34:32,183
from the starting point.

453
00:34:32,184 --> 00:34:36,440
The receiver receives from the satellites, recalculates my location,

454
00:34:36,441 --> 00:34:40,880
but it also did that across the entire path, so it knows the exact steps I took to get

455
00:34:40,881 --> 00:34:42,760
here.

456
00:34:42,761 --> 00:34:45,361
So how can GPS tell us where we are?

457
00:34:45,362 --> 00:34:49,480
By determining the distance to at least three satellites,

458
00:34:49,481 --> 00:34:55,008
the GPS receiver can calculate your location. It's called trilateration.

459
00:34:55,009 --> 00:34:56,620
Once the GPS receiver

460
00:34:56,621 --> 00:35:01,500
calculates how far it is from a satellite, it knows that it must be located somewhere

461
00:35:01,501 --> 00:35:05,292
on the surface of a sphere surrounding that satellite.

462
00:35:05,293 --> 00:35:07,820
If you draw a sphere around a second

463
00:35:07,821 --> 00:35:12,756
satellite, the surfaces of the two spheres intersect at a perfect circle.

464
00:35:12,757 --> 00:35:13,500
The surface

465
00:35:13,501 --> 00:35:19,134
of a sphere around a third satellite will intersect with the circle at two points.

466
00:35:19,135 --> 00:35:19,835
One

467
00:35:19,836 --> 00:35:24,420
of those two points is located on the Earth. And voila, you have your location.

468
00:35:26,620 --> 00:35:31,560
But during the 1990s, the GPS wasn't always so precise and reliable for civilians.

469
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:37,860
There was a selective availability feature, which is an intentional error,

470
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:43,620
so that non-authorized users did not receive as accurate a navigation signal.

471
00:35:44,260 --> 00:35:47,620
In fact, throughout the 90s, this slowly changing random error

472
00:35:47,621 --> 00:35:51,300
would only place you within 100 meters of your location.

473
00:35:51,301 --> 00:35:59,140
President Clinton decided in May of 2000 we are going to turn selective availability

474
00:35:59,141 --> 00:36:00,140
off.

475
00:36:00,141 --> 00:36:07,560
Suddenly, GPS could identify your position to within 10 feet.

476
00:36:07,561 --> 00:36:13,140
At the Shriver Air Force GPS Operations Center in Colorado Springs, Colorado, the 2nd Space

477
00:36:13,141 --> 00:36:19,720
Operations Squadron ensures the satellites are working properly 24-7.

478
00:36:19,721 --> 00:36:24,220
We have satellite system operators do a state of health on a satellite.

479
00:36:24,221 --> 00:36:30,040
We're going to check the pressures, the temperatures, the roll, the pitch, the yaw of that particular

480
00:36:30,041 --> 00:36:35,800
satellite to make sure it's transmitting the best navigation accuracy.

481
00:36:35,801 --> 00:36:40,280
Though originally a military project, the market for civilian GPS receivers has become

482
00:36:40,281 --> 00:36:44,740
three times bigger than its military counterpart.

483
00:36:44,741 --> 00:36:49,820
Just as the GPS found its way out of the desert and into your rental car, another piece of

484
00:36:49,821 --> 00:36:55,260
military equipment used in Operation Desert Storm found its way off the battlefield and

485
00:36:55,261 --> 00:36:58,780
into suburbia.

486
00:36:58,781 --> 00:37:04,480
Following Desert Storm, the AM General Corporation took the military High Mobility Multipurpose

487
00:37:04,481 --> 00:37:10,240
Wheeled Vehicle, or Humvee, and turned it into the Hummer.

488
00:37:10,241 --> 00:37:15,740
The original Hummer, or H1, had the same powertrain, chassis, body and suspension as its military

489
00:37:15,741 --> 00:37:18,840
counterpart.

490
00:37:18,841 --> 00:37:24,760
In 1992, none other than Arnold Schwarzenegger became the first Hummer owner.

491
00:37:24,761 --> 00:37:31,480
But it was in 2003, when a slimmer, less expensive second model Hummer, or H2, hit the market,

492
00:37:31,481 --> 00:37:36,740
that this SUV became more commonplace.

493
00:37:36,741 --> 00:37:40,040
At the Bergstrom Hummer dealership in Milwaukee, Wisconsin,

494
00:37:40,041 --> 00:37:43,740
Hummers are ready to fulfill every soccer mom's commando fantasies.

495
00:37:47,040 --> 00:37:49,840
Alright, to start off with, we're going to be in four high.

496
00:37:49,841 --> 00:37:52,140
So 60% of my power is going to be on the rear wheels,

497
00:37:52,141 --> 00:37:53,840
40% is going to be in the front.

498
00:37:53,841 --> 00:37:55,940
When I come up to our first obstacle,

499
00:37:55,941 --> 00:37:58,540
which is a bunch of rocks like we're going to go off-road,

500
00:37:58,541 --> 00:38:00,340
I'm going to go into four high lock.

501
00:38:00,341 --> 00:38:02,940
That's going to give me equal traction in both wheels,

502
00:38:02,941 --> 00:38:06,140
in all four of them.

503
00:38:06,141 --> 00:38:08,740
It's going to be really bumpy, but we can make it real easily.

504
00:38:10,860 --> 00:38:14,860
With 365 foot-pounds of torque or rotational force,

505
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:20,780
the hefty Hummer can ascend a 60% incline and climb a 13-inch step.

506
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:30,000
The H1 can even ford 30 inches of water because all of the gears are tightly sealed.

507
00:38:30,001 --> 00:38:36,840
But the 8,500-pound off-road vehicle averages only about 10 miles per gallon.

508
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:41,460
And the new millennium isn't the guilt-free, gas-guzzling joyride of the 90s.

509
00:38:42,480 --> 00:38:45,480
So there's a smaller Hummer, the H3.

510
00:38:46,280 --> 00:38:54,320
While the H3 maintains the capabilities of the H1 and H2, it weighs 2,000 pounds less and is nearly a foot and a half shorter in length.

511
00:38:54,321 --> 00:39:01,320
And it gets 18 miles per gallon, nearly double that of the H1.

512
00:39:01,321 --> 00:39:06,320
The Hummer may have brought excitement to some,

513
00:39:06,321 --> 00:39:09,320
but another innovation of the 90s started a type of gaming

514
00:39:09,321 --> 00:39:14,320
that would morph into one of the most lucrative entertainment products of all time.

515
00:39:14,321 --> 00:39:21,320
Geocaching is a worldwide internet-based outdoor treasure hunting game

516
00:39:21,321 --> 00:39:25,800
that uses the global positioning system to hide and seek containers called caches.

517
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:31,320
Today, there are more than 400,000 caches hidden worldwide.

518
00:39:32,440 --> 00:39:35,020
90s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

519
00:39:39,120 --> 00:39:41,240
It's the 26th century.

520
00:39:41,820 --> 00:39:43,520
Alien races have invaded the Earth.

521
00:39:44,540 --> 00:39:48,140
Led by the barrel of a gun, it's up to you to save humanity.

522
00:39:48,141 --> 00:39:57,680
Released on September 25, 2007, Halo 3 is Microsoft's third of the series for the game system Xbox.

523
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:02,500
First-day sales in the U.S. alone reached $170 million,

524
00:40:03,400 --> 00:40:09,040
making it the single-day highest-grossing entertainment product ever.

525
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:16,360
Halo 3 is a so-called first-person shooter game and can trace its roots right back to the 1990s.

526
00:40:16,361 --> 00:40:24,360
It was on May 5, 1992, that computer game developer id Software released Wolfenstein 3D

527
00:40:24,361 --> 00:40:29,360
and shocked gamers across the United States.

528
00:40:29,361 --> 00:40:35,360
When people first saw Wolfenstein 3D, this world just blew them away.

529
00:40:35,361 --> 00:40:39,360
Wolfenstein 3D was a world that you could interact with.

530
00:40:39,361 --> 00:40:43,360
You, as the player, take the role of the main character.

531
00:40:43,361 --> 00:40:47,400
But the 3D world in Wolfenstein was actually anything but.

532
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:53,700
We call it 2.5D, but all the things that you saw were flat.

533
00:40:53,840 --> 00:40:56,280
They were objects drawn. It's what we call sprites.

534
00:40:56,780 --> 00:41:01,460
And so it would be just like sort of a stand-up cardboard cutout of all the monsters.

535
00:41:02,700 --> 00:41:06,960
They were just stretched and scaled in the screen so they could get bigger or smaller,

536
00:41:07,080 --> 00:41:08,880
depending on how far away you were from them.

537
00:41:08,881 --> 00:41:14,340
Wolfenstein 3D may have been one of the original first-person shooters,

538
00:41:14,920 --> 00:41:21,680
but it was id Software's release of Doom in 1993 that really established the genre.

539
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:27,240
And fighting the evil demons and zombies took a little bit of the sting out of killing.

540
00:41:29,080 --> 00:41:33,080
In every way that Wolfenstein 3D was innovative and irreverent,

541
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:37,180
Doom literally just took that and said, you know,

542
00:41:37,181 --> 00:41:39,840
I'll see yours and I'll raise you twice as much.

543
00:41:39,841 --> 00:41:44,180
Unlike Wolfenstein, Doom was much more three-dimensional.

544
00:41:44,181 --> 00:41:47,180
You had different heights and floors.

545
00:41:47,181 --> 00:41:52,320
We even had, you know, this kind of large outdoor landscapes.

546
00:41:52,321 --> 00:41:55,340
One of the things that made Doom so successful

547
00:41:55,341 --> 00:41:57,660
was that it was a fair where, you know,

548
00:41:57,661 --> 00:42:01,300
everybody could download the first episode.

549
00:42:01,301 --> 00:42:03,800
An estimated 10 million people worldwide

550
00:42:03,801 --> 00:42:07,180
downloaded Doom within two years.

551
00:42:07,181 --> 00:42:09,380
And playing the game wasn't the only thing you could do.

552
00:42:11,260 --> 00:42:16,280
Id's John Carmack even designed Doom to allow players to modify and extend the game themselves.

553
00:42:18,280 --> 00:42:23,820
Modifying the game can mean anything from replacing one of the enemies with your boss's picture or something,

554
00:42:24,100 --> 00:42:26,060
up through creating custom levels.

555
00:42:26,440 --> 00:42:32,200
So with Doom, I made the conscious decision that all of the media that we used to create it

556
00:42:32,201 --> 00:42:36,060
could be overridden by anything that somebody adds on top of it.

557
00:42:36,061 --> 00:42:42,060
Since then, users have created thousands of modifications, or mods, for DOOM.

558
00:42:42,061 --> 00:42:45,060
Most can be downloaded for free on the internet.

559
00:42:45,061 --> 00:42:53,060
DOOM really opened up the doors, and it really legitimized PC gaming and our industry,

560
00:42:53,061 --> 00:42:56,060
and launched what we see today.

561
00:42:57,060 --> 00:43:01,060
Of course, video game players wanted a piece of the PC game pie,

562
00:43:01,061 --> 00:43:04,060
but they needed better systems to play them on.

563
00:43:04,061 --> 00:43:08,060
So video game consoles underwent a major makeover,

564
00:43:08,061 --> 00:43:11,060
replacing cartridges with discs.

565
00:43:11,061 --> 00:43:16,060
On December 3, 1994, Sony launched the PlayStation,

566
00:43:16,061 --> 00:43:20,060
the first video game console that could play disc video games.

567
00:43:20,061 --> 00:43:24,060
A game console like the PlayStation is a creative device.

568
00:43:24,061 --> 00:43:29,060
You put your CD into the drive, and from there,

569
00:43:29,061 --> 00:43:36,800
It goes into an entire computer system that runs code that's on that CD.

570
00:43:37,540 --> 00:43:40,240
So the code comes off, loads into memory.

571
00:43:40,440 --> 00:43:44,100
There's a powerful computer on here and a powerful graphics processor

572
00:43:44,101 --> 00:43:49,380
that's able to invent the scenes that will appear on the television screen

573
00:43:49,381 --> 00:43:52,600
based on the input that the user provides through the controller.

574
00:43:53,340 --> 00:43:57,940
It was a 90s technology that has given grown men countless thumb blisters.

575
00:43:59,060 --> 00:44:06,120
Like many technologies of the 90s, you can add it to the list of electronics that have

576
00:44:06,121 --> 00:44:10,740
become a staple in our everyday life.

577
00:44:10,741 --> 00:44:15,100
But at the end of the 90s, as the new millennium approached, panic spread about our dependence

578
00:44:15,101 --> 00:44:16,100
on computers.

579
00:44:16,101 --> 00:44:22,220
Of course, when the ball dropped, our digital world just kept on turning.

